// BUG (2026-07-14, seed rcdzc): a plain `//` LINE COMMENT at the top level HIDES the
// following top-level form. The ML reader wraps a leading line comment as
// `(comment "…" <next-form>)` and the compiler's TOP-LEVEL SCAN does not see through the
// `(comment …)` wrapper — so the wrapped `def`/`type`/`export` becomes invisible and every
// reference to it is an "unbound name `comment`" at the top level (plus the def's own name
// unbound wherever it is used).
//
// This very file demonstrates it: `cdz check` on it reports `unbound name comment` for each
// `//`-led form below and `unbound name f`. Remove the two `//` lines (or turn them into `///`
// doc comments) and it checks clean.
//
// SHARP BOUNDARY (probed 2026-07-14) — contradicts an earlier note that said only @test/effect
// were affected; it is BROADER:
//   - a plain `//` leading ANY top-level form (plain `def`, `type`, `export`) → BROKEN
//   - a `//` BETWEEN two defs, or a `//` TRAILING-INLINE on a def, or a `//` with a blank line
//     after it → still BROKEN (the wrapper attaches to the next top-level form regardless)
//   - a `///` DOC comment on a `def`/`type` → FINE (that path strips the leading doc)
//   - a `//` INSIDE a def body (not at the top level) → FINE
// So the gap is precisely: the top-level scan strips a leading `(doc …)` but NOT a leading
// `(comment …)`. The fix mirrors the doc-strip: peel a leading `(comment …)` in the same scan
// (db.rs, alongside the `(doc …)` handling that scan_type_decl / the def scan already do).
//
// Impact on the port: no source module can carry an ordinary `//` explanatory comment at the
// top level — every comment must be a `///` doc or live inside a body. The manifest reader
// already peels `(comment …)` as a workaround, but `cdz check` on any commented module still
// errors, so it is a real top-level-scan gap.
def f(x: Int64) = x

export { f }
